  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "The tick on top",
      "body": "<p>Most Telugu consonants wear a small check mark on top, the <i>talakattu</i>. It is the built-in vowel a: <span lang=\"te\">క</span> is ka. When you add another vowel, the tick is replaced by that vowel's sign: <span lang=\"te\">కి</span> ki, <span lang=\"te\">కె</span> ke. A few letters, like <span lang=\"te\">ఙ</span> and <span lang=\"te\">ణ</span>, have no tick at all.</p>\n<p>Telugu letters grew round because they were written with a stylus on palm leaves, where straight lines along the grain would split the leaf.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Second forms (vattulu)",
      "body": "<p>When two consonants meet with no vowel between them, the second one is written as a small 'subscript' form under or beside the first. These are called <i>vattulu</i>:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"te\">క్ + క = క్క</td><td>kka, as in <span lang=\"te\">కుక్క</span> (dog)</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"te\">మ్ + మ = మ్మ</td><td>mma, as in <span lang=\"te\">అమ్మ</span> (mother)</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"te\">త్ + ర = త్ర</td><td>tra, as in <span lang=\"te\">యంత్రం</span> (machine)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Most second forms look like a shrunken version of the full letter without its tick. A few, like the one for ర, look quite different.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Words end in vowels",
      "body": "<p>Telugu has been called 'the Italian of the East' because nearly every word ends in a vowel: <span lang=\"te\">ఇల్లు</span> illu, <span lang=\"te\">పాలు</span> pālu. A final m in Sanskrit words is written with the sunna (ం), as in <span lang=\"te\">కమలం</span> kamalaṁ, and said as a soft m or nasal vowel.</p>"
    }
  ]
}
